          ":question/id": "221f7f46-e501-5c4b-9474-541896c7450d",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-167635",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $A=2$ and $B=3,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
