          ":question/problem": "Given that $A=3$ and $B=2,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We found that $A=3$ and $B=2$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
