        ":activity/steps": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184906,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186185366,
              ":tutorial/content": "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x?$ \n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1})+(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed $c_1+c_2=C.$ (The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)",
              ":tutorial/id": "207bbd12-158a-58be-b000-82cace6dcdde",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 701,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "61a29b78-51b2-509a-bb12-5c2009c1ee92",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 1351,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184907
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184907,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184153,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186184057,
                ":question/id": "e4a94ca8-82ee-5776-a46d-b735d4ab60fb",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-371",
                ":question/problem": "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1.$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398",
              ":knowledge-point/key-prerequisites": [
                {
                  ":db/id": 17592186049481
                }
              ],
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"
            },
            ":step/id": "319d1f65-0963-5490-81bd-5f970237cb5a",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 1352,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184908
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184908,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186185367,
              ":tutorial/content": "For more complicated functions consisting of the sum of functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the constant factor and sum rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int k_1 f(x) \\pm k_2 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x = k_1\\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm k_2\\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "1b5ce4c9-ef44-52d9-a506-96aa15055e00",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 703,
              ":tutorial/title": "Combining the Constant Factor and Sum Rules"
            },
            ":step/id": "22856be8-19a5-564b-abcd-7c797b0d4fa8",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 1354,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184909
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184909,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184154,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186184058,
                ":question/id": "ea7dadee-2b44-5e62-bef4-26fb0cb185ea",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-372",
                ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2 x^3 - x \\,\\textrm d x.$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{3}-x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d",
              ":knowledge-point/key-prerequisites": [
